course: dictionary
lesson: 878
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it, which is its context. The word binding can be a noun, an adjective, or a verb, so the clues matter.
explain: «The old book had a cracked binding.» Here binding is the cover and spine that hold the pages together. «The rules were binding, so everyone had to follow them.» Here binding means that they had to be obeyed. The clues are cracked and rules.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the right one. If not, try another meaning or check a dictionary.
fact: Binding means the cover and spine that hold the pages of a book together.
fact: As an adjective, binding means having to be obeyed, as in a «legally binding» promise.
fact: A binding can also be a strip or fastening that holds something, like ski bindings that fix boots to skis.
fact: As a verb, bind means to tie or fasten things together.
fact: Egg is a binding agent in cakes: it holds the mixture together.
exercise: «The old book had a cracked binding.» What does binding mean here? | A loud noise in the hall | A strip of paper for writing notes | *The cover and spine that hold the pages together | A bright colour on the wall
exercise: «The rules were binding, so everyone had to follow them.» What does binding mean here? | Very loose and easy to ignore | Shiny and new | *Having to be obeyed | Hard to find
exercise: What is a binding agent in a cake? | Something that makes it sweeter | A cold place to cool it | A tin to bake it in | *Something that holds the mixture together
test: «She bought ski bindings for her new skis.» What are the bindings? | Her favourite snacks | Bags for carrying the skis | Long poles for the snow | *Fastenings that hold her boots to the skis
test: «The contract was legally binding, so both sides had to keep their promise.» Which phrase means the same as binding here? | Written in pencil | Easy to break | *Must be kept | Very old
exam: In «Fold the paper and bind the pages with thread.» what does bind mean? | To cut into small pieces | To lose count | *To tie or fasten together | To cover in paint
